WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.160
After more than a decade in development,&nbsp;
Disney’s newest action-adventure epic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:07.360
called “Jungle Cruise'' has finally&nbsp;
charted a course into theaters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.760
Before you see it, here’s&nbsp;
everything we know so far.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.800
Back in 2018, it looked like we'd be seeing&nbsp;
"Jungle Cruise" much sooner. As the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:17.760
pushed through its development schedule,&nbsp;
the Walt Disney Company was prepared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.160
roll the film out as early as 2019, and&nbsp;
set an initial release date for October&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:26.000
of that year. That would have pitted the film&nbsp;
against eventual hits like "Joker." However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.360
well before that release date was&nbsp;
upon us, Disney shifted the film.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:32.400
There was some speculation at the time&nbsp;
that "Jungle Cruise" had been moved to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:37.280
occupy a prime theatrical release window that had&nbsp;
previously been reserved for a "Star Wars" film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:41.760
Remember, in 2018 Disney was still purportedly&nbsp;
working toward a goal of releasing a "Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.640
Wars" flick every year, alternating between&nbsp;
main saga stories and spinoffs. Then "Solo:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:51.040
A Star Wars Story" arrived to disappointing&nbsp;
box office returns in May of 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:56.000
setting the stage for a bit of a retooling. By&nbsp;
2019, there were no "Star Wars" films planned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.880
for 2020, so "Jungle Cruise" was tapped in to&nbsp;
compete in the busy summer box office schedule.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:05.920
We got another potential release date after&nbsp;
that first false start: July 24, 2020. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:10.480
going to be a classic sweet summer blockbuster,&nbsp;
just the way things used to be. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:15.680
thanks to the pandemic, that 2020 date has come&nbsp;
and gone. A few rumors circulated that Disney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.320
might go the VOD route with the first entry in&nbsp;
its franchise, but that all proved to be hearsay.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.640
Disney remained committed to a traditional&nbsp;
theatrical release for its adventurous tentpole.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:29.440
Fear not, Disney fans, "Jungle Cruise" will enjoy&nbsp;
a traditional theatrical release on July 30,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.800
2021, and it seems like this&nbsp;
one's really going to take.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.480
That means this release date comes almost&nbsp;
three full years after the formal conclusion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:40.720
of filming back in September 2018. We'll&nbsp;
have to wait for the press junkets to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:44.560
if Johnson and Blunt even remember what&nbsp;
the movie's about. Mark your calendars.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:50.720
After years of development, the first official&nbsp;
trailer finally arrived online in October of 2019.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:54.000
The footage revealed an action-adventure&nbsp;
comedy that owes a lot to previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.120
period pieces in the subgenre.&nbsp;
Think of Disney's own "Pirates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.400
of the Caribbean" and Universal's&nbsp;
"Mummy" franchise from the 2000s.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:07.520
"We are in serious trouble."

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:11.520
The trailer begins by establishing the film&nbsp;
as a period drama set sometime in the early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:15.600
20th century, though not so early that&nbsp;
automobiles aren't prevalent. From there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:20.560
it's more-or-less non-stop action and comedy. We&nbsp;
get to meet our main characters, Lily and Frank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.000
and we see the two of them meet and team&nbsp;
up. But what we don't see a whole lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.840
is the actual jungle cruise the characters&nbsp;
are set to embark on. Still, there's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:32.160
enough here to hint that the trip will soon turn&nbsp;
supernatural with help from an ancient curse.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.160
A second full-length trailer that&nbsp;
came down the pike in 2021 is even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:39.920
more absolutely action-packed. It&nbsp;
keeps the focus on Blunt and Johnson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:43.040
often in pursuit of or being&nbsp;
pursued by some nasty business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:48.400
supernatural or otherwise. It also provides a few&nbsp;
more glimpses of the actual cruise in question.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.920
There's even some Jesse Plemons action&nbsp;
in what looks to be a German U-Boat.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:54.080
“Hallöchen!”

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:58.640
This is likely the final glimpse of the project&nbsp;
before game time, so go ahead and fire it up.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:03.360
"Jungle Cruise" is set to be a rollicking&nbsp;
action-comedy vehicle in the vein of past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.520
Disney hits like "Pirates of the Caribbean." As&nbsp;
with Johnny Depp and the first "Pirates" film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.960
one of the keys to the project's success&nbsp;
is picking a star to frame the film around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:13.120
and then filling out a compelling ensemble.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:16.560
In the case of "Jungle Cruise," the&nbsp;
star role will be filled by not one but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:21.280
two stars with experience in both action and&nbsp;
comedy. The film's leading man is Dwayne Johnson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:28.080
who began circling the movie as far back as&nbsp;
2015, and finally hopped aboard in 2017. In 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.360
it was revealed that Johnson's co-star&nbsp;
for the film would be Emily Blunt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:35.360
who's proven her action chops on films like&nbsp;
"Edge of Tomorrow" and "A Quiet Place."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:40.080
She’s also already had major successes with Disney&nbsp;
in films like "Into the Woods" and "Mary Poppins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:44.640
Returns." Both leads are known commodities, and&nbsp;
by the looks of the trailers released to date,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.760
a lot of the movie's success will&nbsp;
be hanging on their performances.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:52.160
While Johnson and Blunt are the key leads to the&nbsp;
film, they're not the only stars attached. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:56.720
film will also feature "Breaking Bad" and "Fargo"&nbsp;
standout Jesse Plemons in a villainous role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:01.920
In addition, British comedian Jack Whitehall stars&nbsp;
as the brother of Blunt's character. Édgar Ramírez&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.240
plays a mercenary leading another expedition to&nbsp;
the Tree of Life in direct competition with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:10.560
Johnson-Blunt cruise. "Billions" star Paul&nbsp;
Giamatti is involved in a role originally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:15.280
described as a "crusty harbormaster." So then,&nbsp;
it's as star-studded as you might expect from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:18.800
the franchise Disney hopes will become the&nbsp;
successor to "Pirates of the Caribbean."

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:23.360
"Jungle Cruise" is a period film that's&nbsp;
heavy on the kind of action-comedy blend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.000
that inspired classics like&nbsp;
the "Indiana Jones" films.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.640
It's a swashbuckling adventure that will feature&nbsp;
no small number of set pieces and big effects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:34.800
The film follows Lily, a scientist who makes&nbsp;
a shocking discovery in London that could lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:39.760
her to the legendary Tree of Life. The trouble&nbsp;
is, the Tree lies deep in the Amazon rainforest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:44.000
which means she's got to travel to South&nbsp;
America and set herself up with transportation.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:48.000
Once there, she meets Frank, a jungle cruise&nbsp;
captain who seems more interested in conning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.160
tourists out of their money than helping anyone to&nbsp;
find a place that sounds like it's make-believe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:56.080
Lily and her brother have plenty of money&nbsp;
to hire Frank, though, so he takes the job.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:59.200
From there, it's an all-out adventure&nbsp;
into the heart of the Amazon river,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:03.520
but the journey to the Tree of Life won't be&nbsp;
easy. Lily's not the only one trying to find it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:07.920
of course, and it turns out she and Frank&nbsp;
may also be contending with an ancient curse.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:12.560
All the elements are there. The quest. The&nbsp;
elevated, fantasy-historical setting. The stylish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:17.520
visual effects. Heck — there's even an ancient&nbsp;
curse. It really is "Curse of the Black Pearl" on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:22.720
the Amazon River in the best possible ways. There&nbsp;
are certainly worse films to emulate, after all.

00:05:23.676 --> 00:05:26.240
The film's journey to the big&nbsp;
screen begins with the theme park&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:30.240
attraction's opening at Disneyland&nbsp;
in 1955. For our purposes, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.240
the real road to a movie version of the ride&nbsp;
kicked off in the 2000s, after Walt Disney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:38.560
Pictures saw major success with "Pirates of&nbsp;
the Caribbean: The Curse of the Black Pearl."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:42.400
That film, also based on a Disney Parks&nbsp;
attraction, launched an action-adventure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:46.960
franchise for the studio, so Disney started to&nbsp;
mine other possible park rides for film material.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:51.920
"Jungle Cruise" seemed like a natural fit, and in&nbsp;
2006 Disney began trying to nail down a script.

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:56.480
"Jungle Cruise" took what seemed at the time&nbsp;
like a big leap toward the screen back in 2011.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:00.800
At this time, it was reported by EW that Tom&nbsp;
Hanks and Tim Allen, the duo that helped make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:05.360
"Toy Story" a hit, would team up again to star&nbsp;
in the film. That pairing ultimately fizzled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:10.000
and by 2015 Dwayne Johnson was expressing&nbsp;
interest in the project. Johnson was dubbed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:14.960
the film's official star in 2017, and by&nbsp;
2018 "Jungle Cruise" had begun shooting.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:15.760
“Here we go.”

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:21.280
In 2017, with Johnson officially onboard to&nbsp;
star in the film, "Jungle Cruise'' began to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:25.360
set up the rest of its creative team — both&nbsp;
in front of and behind the camera. Johnson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:29.200
as he has in many of his recent blockbusters,&nbsp;
took a rather active role in the process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:34.160
because he's also serving as one of the project's&nbsp;
producers. In the spring of 2017 the film began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:37.920
searching for a director, and Johnson expressed&nbsp;
a preference for "Wonder Woman" helmer Patty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:42.240
Jenkins. Jenkins ultimately stayed at Warner&nbsp;
Bros. to direct a "Wonder Woman" sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:47.440
and by the summer of 2017 Jaume Collet-Serra was&nbsp;
revealed as the director after passing on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:52.000
opportunity to direct a "Suicide Squad" sequel.&nbsp;
That "Suicide Squad" film eventually went to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.560
James Gunn of "Guardians of the Galaxy" fame, so&nbsp;
everyone pretty much ended up where they belonged.

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:01.840
Though "Jungle Cruise" is being positioned by&nbsp;
Disney as a crowd-pleasing action adventure flick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:06.560
the film began generating controversy well&nbsp;
before its release. In August of 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:10.880
Jack Whitehall announced via Instagram that he'd&nbsp;
been cast in the film. It was later revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:15.280
that he'd be playing the brother of Emily Blunt's&nbsp;
character. That same month, a report surfaced from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:19.840
U.K. tabloid The Sun which revealed Whitehall's&nbsp;
character would be among the first major openly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:23.920
gay characters in a Disney production.&nbsp;
Describing his character, the report said,

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:27.600
“[His character is] one who is hugely&nbsp;
effete, very camp and very funny."

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:31.600
The report quickly generated controversy,&nbsp;
not because Disney would feature an openly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:35.840
gay character in the film, but because of how&nbsp;
the character would reportedly be portrayed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:40.800
Many social media users worried that Disney was&nbsp;
simply leaning back on tired gay stereotypes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:45.600
for its first major gay character. Others were&nbsp;
concerned about who would play the role. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:49.600
Whitehall, a straight man, would be playing such&nbsp;
a potentially groundbreaking role for Disney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:52.320
instead of a gay actor, was&nbsp;
also a point of contention.

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:56.640
Disney has not officially responded to the&nbsp;
controversy, but Whitehall has also gained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:01.680
defenders since the outrage. We'll just have to&nbsp;
wait until July 30 to see how it all pans out.

